      <h2 id="evening">One Tuesday night</h2>

      <p>Twenty-two people, four courts, and it is twenty past eight. Court 2 finishes. Someone who arrived at 8:05 still has not played. One of your regulars has just played three on the trot and wants to sit the next one out. Two players have partnered each other twice already.</p>

      <p>On a peg board, you hold all of that in your head while four people wait and look at you.</p>

      <p>ePegboard puts the next game on the screen the moment a court frees up, with the players it has chosen and how even it expects the game to be. You can take it, swap somebody out, lock a player in, or ignore it entirely and build the game yourself.</p>

      <div class="shot" style="margin:22px 0">
        <img src="/img/session-board.png" alt="The ePegboard session board: three courts in play with running timers and live scores, a free court offering Pick and Start, the next game prep panel with swap and lock controls, and the waiting members list with check-in">
      </div>

      <p style="color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.9rem;margin-top:-6px">Courts in play, a free court ready to pick, who is waiting, and the controls to overrule any of it.</p>

      <p style="margin-top:22px">The late arrival comes into consideration because waiting time counts. The player who wants a rest is paused with one tap and comes back in when they say so. The pair who keep being drawn together are steered apart, because recent partners count against a pick. <strong>None of it needs you to remember it.</strong></p>

      <h2 id="what">What it changes about your evening</h2>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>It aims for closer games.</strong> The picker balances the four players on their ratings and shows what it expects the score to be, so more of the evening is worth playing rather than a formality.</li>
        <li><strong>Sitting out is shared out.</strong> Waiting time is part of the decision, so the same three people are less likely to carry the evening's rests.</li>
        <li><strong>The selection can be explained.</strong> The pick is on a screen with its reasoning visible, so "why am I not on?" has an answer that is not just your judgement.</li>
        <li><strong>You get more of your own evening back.</strong> That is the actual point. An organiser who is not running the board all night is an organiser who can be on court.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Practical things that matter on the night: no player accounts are needed, a shared tablet or laptop can run the whole session, late arrivals and early leavers are ordinary rather than disruptive, and you can always overrule the software.</p>

      <h2 id="evidence">Whether clubs actually stay</h2>

      <p>The honest test of any of this is not a feature list, it is whether clubs run a second night.</p>

      <p><strong>Two in three clubs that ran a real first session with ePegboard this year ran another, and getting on for half have run five or more.</strong> Measured on 9 September 2026, counting only sessions in which a game was actually started, and excluding demo and template clubs.</p>

      <p>Separately, we commissioned AI evaluations of thirty club-night products against a rubric written before anything was scored. ePegboard came first in all three runs. We have <a href="/compare">published all of it</a> - the prompts, the scores, the products we could not assess, our own weaknesses, and the places a rival beats us.</p>

      <h2 id="shortlist">Where to see the products scored</h2>

      <p>Once you know which of the decisions above matter at your club, the next question is which products do them well. We scored <strong>32 badminton club-night products</strong> against a published 100-point rubric built on exactly these criteria, and ran the whole evaluation three times to test whether the result held.</p>

