        <p style="margin-top:14px"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="/compare">See the 2026 comparison of 32 products</a></p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="different">Live queue or planned rounds?</h2>

      <p>Before comparing ratings or leaderboards, decide whether the software should plan the evening <strong>as it happens</strong> or <strong>as a sequence of rounds</strong>. It is the decision most comparisons skip and it rules out half the market either way.</p>

      <p>A live system waits for a court to finish, looks at who is available now and chooses the next game. It absorbs late arrivals, early departures and courts finishing at different times. A round-based system generates the whole evening up front: legible, printable, and constantly edited by reality.</p>

      <p>Neither is better. A stable nine-player group may prefer knowing all five games at 7pm. A twenty-plus club with arrivals until 8:15 almost certainly wants live decisions. <strong>ePegboard, ShuttlePilot, Court Manager, ShuttlSync, ShuttleStats, NextFour and QourtX are live; SportRevive plans rounds.</strong></p>

      <p>The second question is worth asking any vendor: <strong>what can outweigh queue order?</strong> Skill, partner repeats, a requested rest? Ask them to explain a case where the longest-waiting player is not picked. The answer tells you how the product actually behaves at 9pm.</p>

      <h2 id="alternatives">Three things ePegboard does not do</h2>

      <p>These are not preferences. They are capabilities we do not have, and if one of them is your actual problem then another product is the better first call.</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Collecting money: ShuttleStats.</strong> If chasing session fees drains more of your week than choosing games does, this is the closer fit. Members book and pay through Stripe with capacity, waitlists and refunds attached, and there is no subscription - the club pays a flat fee per member payment. <strong>ePegboard records what was paid; it does not collect it.</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Running without a reliable internet connection: Court Manager or ShuttlSync.</strong> If your hall has no signal worth trusting and mobile data or a hotspot is not a dependable fallback, you need software that keeps running offline. Court Manager runs on the device and does exactly that, including handling venue login pages that interfere with it; ShuttlSync supports offline sessions. <strong>Our web app needs a connection.</strong> Our only fully offline option is a separate Windows product at GBP 60 a year, and we are no longer developing it - major bug fixes only. If you truly cannot rely on a connection, pick one of those two rather than us.</li>
        <li><strong>A planned evening rather than live picking: SportRevive.</strong> If your numbers are stable and you would rather hand out the whole night's rounds at 7pm, that is a different model and we do not offer it. See the section above on which one your club actually wants.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>If several of those are your problem at once - subs, membership records and court booking together - then what you are shopping for is <a href="/badminton-club-management-software">badminton club management software</a> rather than a tool for running the night, and the shortlist above is the wrong shortlist to be reading.</p>

      <h2 id="others">Other tools worth comparing</h2>

      <p>These solve the same problem we do, in their own way. We would put ePegboard beside any of them, and none of them is a reason on its own to look past it.</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>ShuttlePilot</strong> - phone-first and free while in beta. It is the only product we found that equalises sit-outs <em>across sessions</em> rather than only within tonight, which is worth knowing if the same people always seem to miss out. No published price after beta, and no shared court display.</li>
        <li><strong>ShuttlSync</strong> - browser-based, with a rating that moves with results, seasons and achievements. Free to two live courts, GBP 9.99 a month to four, which is a ceiling worth checking against your club's size.</li>
        <li><strong>NextFour</strong> - keeps the pegboard metaphor closest to a physical board, on phones. Core nights are free; pegboard and skill-based picking sit behind paid tiers bought through the app stores.</li>
        <li><strong>Court Manager</strong> - the most direct peg-board replacement in the set, with unusually well documented fairness rules. Ranks are typed in by hand and never move on their own.</li>
        <li><strong>QourtX</strong> - built across racquet sports, with membership and venue operations around the session. Worth one extra check before a committee budgets: its badminton page and its main pricing page describe different commercial models, so ask for the applicable plan in writing.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="limits">Other limitations worth knowing</h2>

      <p>Beyond the three above, from the same evaluations we published:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Competitions are handicap doubles.</strong> Singles and team formats are not built.</li>
        <li><strong>We publish the picker's signals but not their weights</strong>, so you cannot tune the balance between a close game and a long wait yourself.</li>
        <li><strong>We optimise for a tablet or laptop.</strong> It runs on a phone, but a bigger screen gives the better experience. ShuttlePilot is built specifically for running the night one-handed from a phone.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="test">If you want to test it properly</h2>

      <p>Do not test with eight well-behaved imaginary players. Run a real night and make it awkward: a late arrival after several games, someone sitting out and rejoining, two courts finishing together, a pairing you want kept, a suggestion you overrule, and a wrong score that needs correcting.</p>

      <p>Then ask the one question that matters: <strong>did this take decisions out of my head, or just move them onto a screen?</strong></p>

      <p>Also test leaving. Export whatever the product says belongs to your club. A system can be free to enter and expensive to leave if years of results cannot come with you.</p>

